    description: 'LinkedIn MCP Server gives an AI assistant read and write access to LinkedIn through your own logged-in browser session rather than through the LinkedIn API, which is what makes it usable without a partner-tier API approval. The leading implementation is stickerdaniel/linkedin-mcp-server (3,100+ stars, Apache-2.0), an independent community project that is not affiliated with, endorsed by or sponsored by LinkedIn Corporation or Microsoft. Install it with uvx — configure your client to run `uvx mcp-server-linkedin@latest` — and the maintainers specifically ask you not to pin a version: LinkedIn changes its page structure often, fixes ship in frequent releases, and a pinned build quietly rots into returning empty or wrong data. On first launch the server prepares a shared Patchright Chromium cache under ~/.linkedin-mcp/patchright-browsers and opens a login window on the first tool call that needs authentication; `--login` creates the session explicitly, and `--import-from-browser` reuses an existing LinkedIn session from Chrome, Chromium, Brave, Edge, Arc, Vivaldi, Helium, Yandex or Naver Whale so you can skip the manual sign-in entirely. The tool surface covers profiles (get_person_profile and get_my_profile with explicit section selection across experience, education, skills, certifications, projects, contact info and posts), connections (connect_with_person), messaging (get_inbox, get_conversation, search_conversations, send_message — which requires confirmation), companies (get_company_profile, get_company_posts, search_companies, get_company_employees), jobs (search_jobs, get_job_details, get_saved_jobs), and search and feed reading (search_people by keyword, location, connection degree and current company; get_feed; search_posts with a past-24h/week/month recency filter). It runs over stdio by default and can also serve Streamable HTTP, with Docker and a Claude Desktop MCP bundle as alternative install routes. Because it drives a real session, treat rate of use as an account-safety question, not just a performance one.',
    author: 'stickerdaniel',
