    slug: 'zen-mcp-server',
    name: 'Zen MCP Server',
    description: 'multi-model orchestration for a single coding CLI — formerly Zen MCP, renamed upstream to PAL MCP (a Provider Abstraction Layer). Start with that naming, because it is the first thing anyone searching for "zen mcp server" needs to know: the project now lives at github.com/BeehiveInnovations/pal-mcp-server. The old Zen MCP name still carries most of the search traffic and the repo redirects, but every current command, package path and doc reference uses `pal`, so a config copied from an old blog post will not match the current README. What it does: PAL (formerly Zen) is a Python MCP server that turns your single coding CLI into a multi-model team. You keep working in Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Qwen Code CLI or Cursor, and PAL routes individual sub-tasks out to Gemini, OpenAI, Anthropic, Grok, Azure OpenAI, OpenRouter, DIAL, Ollama or an on-device model — inside a single prompt, with conversation threading so context carries across both tools and models. That threading is the real feature rather than the model list: Gemini can recall what O3 said ten steps earlier, and a documented "context revival" trick lets you continue a conversation after your primary CLI\'s context window resets by having another model remind it of the discussion. The tool surface is a set of guided, multi-step workflows rather than one-shot calls. Enabled by default: `clink` (the CLI-to-CLI bridge), `chat`, `thinkdeep`, `planner`, `consensus`, `codereview`, `precommit`, `debug`, `apilookup` and `challenge`. Disabled by default to save context window: `analyze`, `refactor`, `testgen`, `secaudit`, `docgen` and `tracer` — you enable them by removing names from the `DISABLED_TOOLS` env list. Two of those deserve calling out. `clink` launches an isolated instance of another CLI from inside your current one — Claude Code spawning a Codex subagent for a security audit, for example — with a `role` such as `planner` or `codereviewer`, so heavy investigation happens in a fresh context and only the final report comes back. `challenge` exists purely to stop sycophantic "you\'re absolutely right!" agreement and force critical analysis. `consensus` runs an actual debate between models with stance steering. Prerequisites are Python 3.10+, Git and uv. The recommended install clones the repo and runs `./run-server.sh`, which handles setup, reads API keys from your system environment and auto-configures Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI and Qwen CLI; the faster path is a uvx entry in `.mcp.json` running `uvx --from git+https://github.com/BeehiveInnovations/pal-mcp-server.git pal-mcp-server` with your provider keys under `env`. Any provider with credentials in `.env` activates automatically. Apache 2.0, ~11.7k stars, 1k forks. One maintenance caveat stated plainly: the last push to the repository was 2025-12-15, so check recent commit activity yourself before adopting it for anything critical.',
